        <div id="solids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Does Solids Content Mean in Garage Floor Epoxy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solids content is the share of the liquid that is still on the floor once the coating cures, and it is the best single predictor of how long a garage epoxy lasts. Kits are marketed by the area they cover, yet the thing you are actually paying for is the material left behind. A water-based kit can be close to half water, and that water leaves as it dries. A high-solids or 100% solids kit keeps almost all of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spread one gallon of each across the same 250 square feet and the thick product leaves a film several times heavier. That difference in thickness is what separates a floor showing tire lanes after two years from one that still looks uniform after eight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do you find the solids figure?</h3>

          <p>
            The box often leaves it off, but the technical data sheet nearly always has it. Search for the product name with "TDS" or "data sheet" and look for a line reading "volume solids" or "solids by volume." When a kit lists only coverage and dry time, treat it as a thin product. Price per covered square foot is another tell: water-based kits come in around 50 cents to a dollar per square foot in materials, while thick systems commonly cost two to four dollars.
          </p>

