          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a thicker epoxy always the better buy?</h3>

          <p>
            No. High-solids epoxy gels sooner, so it gives you less room for error, and its thickness exaggerates whatever is wrong with the surface underneath. On a slab with scaling, pitting or a wavy trowel finish, a thin water-based coat follows the contours, while a thick coat collects in the dips and runs thin over the ridges. Either patch and grind before you coat, or pick the thinner kit and accept that it will need attention sooner.
          </p>

          <p>
            A workable rule of thumb: one car parked on a sound slab, with a clean look as the goal, is water-based territory. A creeper, a rolling floor jack, a heavy tool chest or two vehicles parked every day call for a high-solids system, or for the RockSolid hybrid when getting the garage back quickly matters most. Our <a href="/best-garage-floor-coating/">garage floor coating comparison</a> covers the non-epoxy chemistries, polyaspartic included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-part" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is One-Part Epoxy Floor Paint Real Epoxy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not in the way that matters on a garage floor. A true two-part epoxy ships as a resin and a hardener in separate containers, and combining them starts a chemical reaction that turns the liquid into a cross-linked solid. That reaction is why the cured film is hard, shrugs off solvents and cannot be thinned once it has started to set. A one-part "epoxy" floor paint is an acrylic or latex coating with some epoxy ester blended in, and it dries as its water evaporates, exactly like wall paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does the difference show up under tires?</h3>

          <p>
            Rubber that has just come off the road is warm, and warm rubber softens a one-part film enough to grip it. When the car backs out, the tire can take a patch of paint along with it. Two-part coatings hold up to this far better, though no product is immune if the bond to the concrete was weak to begin with. For a floor you park on every day, the extra money and labor of a two-part kit is repaid quickly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is one-part paint the smarter buy?</h3>

          <p>
            One-part products are cheap, can go down one section at a time, come in a wide range of tintable colors and are simple to touch up. A basement workshop, a garage used purely for storage or a floor whose car stays in the driveway is often better served by paint than by a kit. Our guide to <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">concrete floor paint</a> goes deeper on that category.
          </p>

          <p>
            To tell the two apart, ignore the front of the can and read the directions. If you are told to combine two containers and given a pot life in minutes or hours, it is a genuine two-part system. If you just stir and roll, it is a one-part paint, whatever the label calls it. Neither is a con; they are different tools built for different floors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-many-kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Epoxy Kits Do You Need for a Two-Car Garage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A two-car garage of about 400-500 square feet usually takes two water-based kits plus one spare. Car counts on the front of a box are only a rough guide, because garages vary a lot in size. Measure wall to wall, multiply length by width, and subtract any floor under built-in cabinets that will stay put. A typical one-car garage comes to about 240-300 square feet and a three-car often 600-750. Compare that figure with the square footage printed on the kit, not the number of cars.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does the label coverage come up short?</h3>

          <p>
            Porous concrete soaks up coating. The coverage on a kit assumes a reasonably dense slab with a proper profile. A freshly ground or acid-etched floor, an old dusty slab or a broom finish absorbs more, sometimes 20 to 30 percent more on the first coat. Running dry with a third of the floor still bare is the worst case, since a fresh batch will not blend cleanly into a coat that has already begun to cure.
          </p>

