          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the batch number matter?</h3>

          <p>
            It can. Color sometimes drifts slightly from one production batch to the next, and the change shows along the line where one kit ends and the next begins. Buy every kit from the same batch when you can and keep them together until the job starts. Most home centers accept an unopened spare back, so the extra kit is inexpensive insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            A clear topcoat is a separate line on the shopping list; most water-based base kits do not carry enough clear for a full second layer. Direct-sold high-solids kits are often packaged by garage size with the topcoat included, so check exactly what each box contains. Our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/">epoxy garage floor cost guide</a> totals a full two-car job once tools, crack filler and extras are counted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Prep a Garage Floor for Epoxy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the slab for moisture, strip out every trace of oil, then open the surface by grinding or etching until water soaks in. Epoxy holds on mechanically, so it needs a clean surface with texture, known as a profile, to key into. The International Concrete Repair Institute publishes the scale used to describe profile, and garage coatings usually aim for something that feels like 120-grit sandpaper. Power-troweled garage slabs tend to be smooth and dense, which is why a coating can look flawless for a month and then come up in sheets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you check the slab for moisture?</h3>

          <p>
            Tape down an 18-by-18-inch square of clear plastic sheeting on bare concrete, seal all four edges, and leave it for 24 hours. Droplets under the sheet or a darker patch of concrete mean vapor is rising through the slab, and that pressure will eventually push a coating loose. Put any film coating on hold until the moisture is dealt with, or use a vapor-tolerant primer the coating maker approves for the conditions you found.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is degreasing really necessary before etching?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, because acid does not cut oil and epoxy will not bond to it. A concrete degreaser and a stiff brush deal with recent drips; deep, old stains can need a poultice, and our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers the process. Only then should you open the surface. Citric or acid etching works on many older, porous slabs. On smooth or previously sealed floors, a rented diamond grinder with a vacuum attachment is far more dependable, and it is the step most often skipped by owners whose floors later peel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you know the profile is ready?</h3>

          <p>
            Use the water drop check. Flick a few drops onto the prepared concrete. If they soak in within about a minute, the surface is open enough to coat. If they bead, something is still sealing that spot, such as a curing compound, old sealer or leftover residue, so keep working it. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-apply-garage-floor-epoxy/">applying garage floor epoxy</a> lays out the whole sequence, crack repair included, one step at a time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="conditions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Temperature Do You Need to Apply Garage Floor Epoxy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most garage epoxies list an application range of roughly 50-90 degrees Fahrenheit, and the number that counts is the temperature of the concrete, not the air. A slab stays cold long after the air has warmed, particularly in spring. An infrared thermometer costs less than a single kit and ends the guessing. Aim for concrete in the 60s or low 70s, with steady weather forecast for the following day or two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does the dew point matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Dew point is as important as temperature. When the slab sits within about 5 degrees of the dew point, moisture condenses on the surface, which can leave a cloudy, greasy film called amine blush on some epoxies or simply weaken the bond. Plenty of free weather apps show the dew point; check it against your slab reading before mixing. A morning coat on a cold slab followed by a humid afternoon is a classic setup for trouble.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical label numbers to check</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50-90°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common application range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum slab margin above dew point</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 hr</p>
