              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usual wait before parking</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is pot life, and how do you stretch it?</h3>

          <p>
            Pot life is how long a mixed batch stays usable in the container. Heat cuts it short, and so does leaving the mix in a deep pail where the reaction feeds on its own warmth. Get the mixed material into a roller tray or onto the floor without delay, and mix only as much as you can spread inside the label's window. Once a batch starts to thicken or feel warm in the pail, stop using it, because rolling half-gelled epoxy leaves ridges that never flatten out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long before you can walk and park on it?</h3>

          <p>
            Label cure times assume moderate temperatures, and in a cool garage they stretch, sometimes by days. Foot traffic is usually fine after about a day and vehicles after three to seven days, but your product's label rules, and a chilly garage needs extra time on top. Parking on an under-cured floor is one of the surest ways to end up with permanent tire marks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flakes-topcoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Do You Need Flakes and a Clear Topcoat on Epoxy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither is mandatory, but both earn their place on most garage floors, and a topcoat matters most over a thin water-based base. Decorative vinyl flakes are tossed by hand into the wet base coat, and they do three useful things: they hide dust, small stains and roller marks; they break up the surface so it is less slick; and in a full-broadcast system, where flakes blanket the floor and the surplus is scraped off, they add body that disguises an imperfect slab. A light sprinkle is mostly for looks, while heavy coverage changes how the floor both looks and feels.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you broadcast flakes evenly?</h3>

          <p>
            Toss them up and outward so they fall like rain rather than landing in clumps. Stay inside the broadcast window the label gives, which is usually while the base is still wet enough to hold them, and practice on a sheet of cardboard first. Most kits contain enough flakes for a light-to-medium scatter; heavier coverage means buying extra separately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a clear topcoat add?</h3>

          <p>
            A clear coat locks the flakes in place, raises the gloss and gives the floor a sacrificial layer to wear through first. Water-based base kits gain the most because their film is thin. If sunlight reaches the floor through an open door or windows, pick a UV-stable topcoat, since standard epoxy yellows in sun over time. Polyaspartic and urethane clears hold their color well; plain epoxy clears cost less but can amber.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will a glossy floor be slippery?</h3>

          <p>
            It can be. A wet, smooth coating turns slick, so ask for or buy an anti-slip additive to stir into the final coat. It is a fine grit that adds grip without making the floor hard to mop, and on a floor where tires bring in rain and snow it should be treated as essential.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hot-tire" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Causes Hot-Tire Pickup on an Epoxy Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hot-tire pickup happens when the coating's grip on the tire beats its grip on the concrete. Warm rubber softens the film slightly and holds onto it, and as the tire cools and shrinks it pulls. If the coating is bonded to the tire more strongly than to the slab, it leaves with the car. Owners commonly report it within the first months after an install, usually in the exact spot where they park.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are the three main causes?</h3>

          <p>
            In order of importance: weak surface prep, a soft coating and a film that has not finished curing. Grinding produces a stronger bond than acid etching on most slabs. Two-part coatings resist pickup better than one-part paints, and high-solids or hybrid products better than thin water-based films. Waiting out the full cure before parking makes a real difference, especially in a cool garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tires vary too. Performance summer tires and new rubber run softer and warmer. If you drive a performance car, or commute on the highway and pull straight into the garage, weigh that when choosing and lean toward a high-solids system with a tough topcoat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you patch a spot that has already lifted?</h3>

          <p>
            Patches rarely hold for long, because the bond around a failed spot is usually weak as well. The lasting repair is to grind off the affected tire lane and recoat it. If tire marks are your main worry, interlocking tiles sidestep the problem altogether; see our ranking of <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">garage floor tiles</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-choice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Should You Not Epoxy a Garage Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip a film coating when the slab is damp, badly damaged, or about to change hands, or when you cannot spare the time to prep it properly. Epoxy is a film, and a film needs a dry, sound, clean base. A slab that fails the plastic-sheet moisture check, shows efflorescence (white, chalky deposits) coming through, or sits on a poorly drained site is likely to blister or peel however carefully the coating goes down. Vapor-tolerant primers exist, but they add cost and still require the moisture level to be checked against the maker's limit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What slab damage rules epoxy out?</h3>

          <p>
            Wide structural cracks that open and close with the seasons, heavy spalling from road salt, or a slab that is heaving will crack any rigid coating laid over them. Repair the concrete first or go with a floating floor. Interlocking tiles sit on top without bonding and tolerate imperfect concrete, which is why so many older garages end up with them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about rentals and homes you plan to sell?</h3>

          <p>
            Something simpler often serves them better. A penetrating densifier or a clear sealer from our list of <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealers</a> keeps dust down and helps stains wipe away, with almost no chance of a peeling patch that a buyer notices on the first walk-through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be honest about your own time as well. A proper DIY epoxy job, prep included, takes most of two weekends, and the garage is out of action for days afterward. If that does not fit your schedule, a professional installer or a product with a faster return to service is worth the money. Settle that question before you buy the first box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Do You Clean and Maintain an Epoxy Garage Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweep weekly, mop with a mild pH-neutral cleaner, wipe spills promptly and pad anything heavy. Easy cleaning is the main reward of a coated floor. Grit carried in under tires and shoes is what slowly dulls the surface, so a weekly pass with a broom or dust mop matters more than anything else. Mop with warm water and the mild cleaner, avoid strong citrus or vinegar solutions, and skip soap-based products that leave a slippery residue.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which spills can damage cured epoxy?</h3>

          <p>
            Cured epoxy copes well with motor oil and antifreeze, but brake fluid, some solvents and battery acid can soften or stain it when left for hours. Keep shop towels and a bottle of cleaner by the workbench. In winter, park over a mat to catch salty slush, and squeegee standing water toward the door or drain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you protect the floor from point loads?</h3>

          <p>
            Put plywood pads under jack stands, fit glides under heavy cabinets and lay a mat where you kneel and drop tools. Dragging a steel tool chest across the floor will scratch any coating, however tough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you refresh an epoxy floor instead of redoing it?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually, if the base coat is still well bonded. After a few years, a light scuff-sand and a fresh clear topcoat bring back the gloss and add life for far less than a full redo. A pressure washer on a low setting with a wide nozzle helps deep-clean the garage before recoating, and our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer guide</a> lists models suited to the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write down what went on the floor: product name, color, batch number and date. When a section needs repair years later, that note tells you exactly what to buy and which topcoats are compatible. Keep a small bag of leftover flakes too, so a repaired spot blends in instead of showing as a bare gray square in the middle of a speckled floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Garage Floor Epoxy
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the kit's etch packet on a smooth slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The citric etch packed with many kits is gentle, and on a dense, power-troweled floor it may hardly open the surface. If water still beads afterward, the coating has nothing to grip. A day's rental of a diamond grinder with a vacuum shroud costs less than redoing a peeled floor and leaves a far more even profile.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving out the plastic-sheet moisture check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vapor rising through a slab stays invisible until the coating blisters. Taping down plastic for 24 hours costs next to nothing. If the concrete darkens or water gathers under the sheet, stop there, and fix the moisture or use a vapor-tolerant primer the coating maker approves before any film goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing an entire kit at once on a hot day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mixed epoxy makes its own heat, and a full pail in a warm garage can gel long before the label's pot life runs out. Mix partial batches where the system allows it, get the material onto the floor or into a tray quickly, and lay out the job so the final section still gets a wet edge without a rush.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering exactly the label's coverage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Freshly profiled, porous concrete drinks far more on the first coat than the label assumes, sometimes 20 to 30 percent more. Running out partway leaves a visible seam where a new batch meets a coat that is already setting. Buy at least one spare kit, keep the receipt and return it unopened if it goes unused.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving onto the floor before it has cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A coating can feel hard underfoot well before it has fully cured, especially in a cold garage. Warm tires on a soft film are a textbook cause of hot-tire marks. Wait the full time on the label, which is usually three to seven days, add a few more when the slab is cold, and keep the car outside until then.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an epoxy garage floor last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Film thickness and prep decide most of it. A water-based kit over a well-prepared slab typically lasts about three to five years under daily parking before it needs a refresh. High-solids and 100% solids systems laid over a ground floor with a topcoat often go a decade or longer. Poor prep can cut either one down to a matter of months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a water-based kit like EpoxyShield enough for a two-car garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For many households it is, provided the slab is sound, dry and properly profiled. You get a clean look and simple cleanup. It struggles with heavy wear: creepers, jacks, studded tires and constant traffic thin it faster than a high-solids system. Budget for a topcoat or a refresh after a few years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to grind the floor, or will acid etching do?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Etching can be enough on older, porous slabs that absorb water fast. On smooth, dense or previously sealed concrete it often leaves too little texture. Grinding is the more dependable method and the one most professional installers use. If water beads on your floor after cleaning, plan to grind rather than etch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can epoxy go over an old painted or coated garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when the old coating is the same type, firmly bonded and properly scuff-sanded, and the new product's label permits it. Peeling or flaking paint has to come off first, usually by grinding. A two-part epoxy over a weak one-part paint just ties the new coat to a layer that is going to fail. If in doubt, grind down to bare concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after epoxy can I drive on the garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most two-part garage kits call for roughly three to seven days before vehicles at moderate temperatures, and polycuramine or polyaspartic systems can be quicker. A cool slab slows the cure, so allow extra time in spring and fall. Walking on it is usually fine after about a day. The specific label always outranks a general rule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my epoxy garage floor slippery when wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A smooth, glossy coating has almost no texture, so water and slush make it slick. The cure is an anti-slip additive, a fine grit stirred into the topcoat or final coat, or a heavier flake broadcast that leaves texture behind. On a floor that is already finished, a new topcoat with additive adds grip without starting over.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a professional epoxy install worth it over a DIY kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A professional job costs several times more, but it usually covers grinding, crack repair and a thick multi-layer system, often polyaspartic, backed by a warranty. If the slab has problems, you lack time for proper prep, or you want a floor that lasts a decade or more, a pro is often worth it. For a sound slab and a modest budget, a DIY kit makes sense.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

