        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield is the sensible starting point for most people coating a sound one- or two-car slab, provided the floor is ground or thoroughly etched and a spare kit is on hand. Daily parkers, home mechanics and anyone who needs the garage back quickly should spend the extra on RockSolid Polycuramine. If a grinder is already rented and the goal is something close to a professional floor, a thick-build system from ArmorPoxy or Epoxy-Coat puts down far more film per coat.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever the kit, the slab settles the result. Check for moisture, get rid of every trace of oil, cut a real profile, keep an eye on the dew point and let the coating cure fully before parking. Do those five things and even a budget kit looks good for years; skip them and the priciest bucket on the shelf will peel just the same.
          </p>
